+  byte-identical: scip is a flat exhaustive symbol table; v5/v6 model callable
+  arrow-types in the type graph + exclude value-consts from TypeF. scip's `#`
+  suffix lumps interface/class/enum/alias (can't sub-discriminate; its kind field is
+  Unspecified) — so v6 is MORE granular on type-kind than scip. The real gate is
+  occurrence/resolution parity (commit 4 ratchet), not raw symbol diff.
+- Commit 2b's callable entities are SKELETONS: kinded but missing the arrow-type
+  payload + edges that justify their presence in TypeF. Honest sequencing gap, not
+  a bug. Surfaced by the user's "are these good or bad" question.
